        <body>
            <div xml:id="d549">

                <pb facs="0919" n="849" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg849"/>

                <head>No. 550.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to Mr.
                    Wallace.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-06-14"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 14,
                        1883</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 88.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Your dispatch No. 220, of May 22 last, has
                    been received, and the announced decision of the council of state presents the
                    matter of the petroleum concession in a grave light. It is hoped that your
                    proposed direct intercession with the Sultan may result in such a course being
                    adopted by the Turkish Government as will conform to the letter and spirit of
                    existing international engagements. You will, of course, keep the Department
                    advised of all that transpires, and especially of the views of your Russian and
                    Roumanian colleagues upon the subject.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>FRED’K T. FRELINGrHUYSEN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
